## LEG 3 — AUTHORED STEP 1: traffic forwarded, tables correct, and the SOURCE is the subject

  The (S,G) include join, with the new tool:

    tools/tb sudotool tb277 ckt1406c igmp eth4 join 232.5.5.5 10.38.22.70 00:11:22:33:44:77 \
         3 0.5 -3 --source 10.38.22.40
    -> IGMPv3 join 232.5.5.5 rtype=to-include(3) sources=[10.38.22.40] x3 ... -> 224.0.0.22

  **>Check IGMP Groups** and **>Check IGMP Host Joined the correct multicast source**:

    swi_a#show ip igmp groups
    Group Address    Interface            Uptime   Expires  Last Reporter
    232.5.5.5        vlan48               00:00:07  stopped 10.38.22.70
    swi_a#show ip igmp groups detail
    Interface:      vlan48          Group: 232.5.5.5
    Group mode:     Include ()                      <- INCLUDE mode, not exclude
    Last reporter:  10.38.22.70
    Include Source List :
      Source Address  Uptime    v3 Exp    Fwd  Flags
      10.38.22.40     00:00:08  stopped   Yes  <- the correct source, and it is FORWARDING

  **>Check Multicast Table** (both authored read-backs, the second with its name corrected):

    swi_a#show ip mroute
    (10.38.22.40, 232.5.5.5), uptime 00:00:37, stat expires 00:02:53
    Owner PIM-SM, Flags: TF
      Incoming interface: vlan61        Outgoing interface list: vlan48 (1)
    swi_a#show ip pim sparse-mode mroute
    (*,*,RP) Entries: 0    (*,G) Entries: 0    (S,G) Entries: 1    (S,G,rpt) Entries: 0
    (10.38.22.40, 232.5.5.5)
    RPF nbr: 10.0.61.1     RPF idx: vlan61     SPT bit: 1
    Upstream State: JOINED      Local 1      Joined 0      Outgoing 1

  That is textbook SSM and it is what distinguishes this case from T43863: **zero (*,G) entries
  and zero (S,G,rpt) entries**, one (S,G), SPT bit set, RPF straight at the source's router —
  no shared tree, no RP, no Register, in a case where no RP is configured at all.

  **Traffic forwarded**, with the two controls that make it an SSM measurement rather than a
  multicast one, all three flows in ONE leg:

    tools/tb sudotool tb277 ckt1406c arpleg --tag s3-ssm-sg --out /tmp/ckt1406c/T43865 \
      --iface eth3 --watch eth4 \
      --udp '232.5.5.5:5001@10.38.22.40,232.5.5.5:5001@10.38.22.44,232.9.9.9:5001@10.38.22.40' \
      --icmp 10.38.22.65 --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 \
      --sender-ip 10.38.22.40 --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 \
      --count 10 --gap 0.25 --settle 3 --wait 7 --filter "ip"

    cap s3-ssm-sg-eth3.pcap: 40 frame(s)      (all four flows delivered to the DUT)
      x10 UDP 10.38.22.40 -> 232.5.5.5 :5001 [ours] [eth ... -> 01:00:5e:05:05:05]
      x10 UDP 10.38.22.44 -> 232.5.5.5 :5001 [ours] [eth ... -> 01:00:5e:05:05:05]
      x10 UDP 10.38.22.40 -> 232.9.9.9 :5001 [ours] [eth ... -> 01:00:5e:09:09:09]
      x10 ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours]
    cap s3-ssm-sg-eth4.pcap: 21 frame(s)
      x10 ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours] [eth 88:9d:98:df:26:02 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f]
      x9  UDP 10.38.22.40 -> 232.5.5.5 :5001 [ours] [eth 88:9d:98:df:26:02 -> 01:00:5e:05:05:05]

  => the JOINED CHANNEL (10.38.22.40, 232.5.5.5): **9 of 10** (1 consumed by the tree build;
     later legs measured 10 of 10 steady).
  => the SAME GROUP from a DIFFERENT SOURCE (10.38.22.44, 232.5.5.5): **0 of 10**.  This is the
     defining property of SSM and it cannot be shown by any (*,G) test.  Note both flows share
     the link-layer address 01:00:5e:05:05:05, so the discrimination is at layer 3, by source.
  => a DIFFERENT GROUP from the joined source (10.38.22.40, 232.9.9.9): **0 of 10**.
  VERDICT authored step 1: **PASS** — "Multicast routing table updates accordingly", and the
  host joined, and was served by, the correct multicast source only.

