## LEG 5 — AUTHORED STEP 3: interface down/up.  TWO readings, because they differ

  Re-joined first and confirmed forwarding (cap s6-rejoin-eth4.pcap: 9/10 channel + 10/10
  instrument control).  Then:

  (a) **the RECEIVER'S PORT down** — DUT `port1.0.48`, the host's own access port:
      awplus(config-if)#shutdown
      awplus#show interface port1.0.48 status -> port1.0.48 ... disabled
      swi_a#show ip igmp groups
      232.5.5.5        vlan48               00:00:45  stopped 10.38.22.70   <- STILL THERE
      swi_a#show ip mroute
      (10.38.22.40, 232.5.5.5) ... Incoming interface: vlan61
        Outgoing interface list: vlan48 (1)                                 <- STILL THERE
      => **the multicast table does NOT update.**  That is correct, not a fault, and the reason
      is the topology: `port1.0.48` is an L2 access port **on the DUT**, while the multicast
      router is swi_a, whose `vlan48` SVI is still up over the trunk.  swi_a has no way to learn
      that a host two switches away unplugged, and the membership is soft state that must time
      out.  Worth stating because the authored expectation ("Multicast routing table updates
      accordingly") is only met for the reading in (b) — a tester who shuts the host's port and
      expects the table to clear will record a false failure.  Recorded as **O-28**.
      (`no shutdown` restored it: `port1.0.48 ... connected a-full a-1000`.)

  (b) **the ROUTER'S INTERFACE down** — swi_a `interface vlan48`, the multicast interface:
      swi_a(config-if)#shutdown
      swi_a#show ip igmp groups -> (header only; empty)
      swi_a#show ip mroute      -> IP Multicast Routing Table   (empty)
      swi_a#show ip pim sparse-mode interface
      Total configured interfaces: 2   Maximum allowed: 1024
      Total active interfaces:     1                      <- only vlan61 left
      10.0.61.2        vlan61    4        v2/S   1
      => **both tables update immediately**, and PIM drops the interface from its active set.

  (c) **and back UP**, with a re-join:
      swi_a(config-if)#no shutdown
      cap s7-ifup-recovered-eth4.pcap: 20 frame(s)
        x10 ICMP t8/c0 ... (10/10 instrument control)
        x9  UDP 10.38.22.40 -> 232.5.5.5 :5001 [ours] [eth 88:9d:98:df:26:02 -> 01:00:5e:05:05:05]
        (and 0 of 10 for the wrong source 10.38.22.44, carried in the same leg)
      swi_a#show ip mroute
      (10.38.22.40, 232.5.5.5), uptime 00:00:22 ... Outgoing interface list: vlan48 (1)
      => state and dataplane both recover, and **SSM source filtering is still enforced after
         the interface flap**.
  VERDICT authored step 3: **PASS**, with the two readings distinguished.

## LEG 6 (designed, satisfactory-level) — removal.  It produced finding F-14

  First the syntax, because the negation is asymmetric (same family as group A's O-10):
    awplus(config)#no ip pim ssm default
    % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.
    awplus(config)#no ip pim ssm  ?        <- read-only probe
      <cr>
  i.e. the positive form takes `default|range <acl>` and **the negation takes neither** — bare
  `no ip pim ssm`.  Recorded as **O-27**.

  With `no ip pim ssm` applied on BOTH routers and `ip pim ssm default` verifiably gone:
    awplus#show running-config | include pim
    service pim
     ip pim sparse-mode
     ip pim sparse-mode                    <- no `ip pim ssm` line anywhere
  the existing channel kept forwarding 10 of 10 and the (S,G) survived.  That is **not** a
  defect: an IGMPv3 (S,G) include join is legitimate in plain PIM-SM too and joins the SPT
  directly, so removing the SSM range need not tear it down.  But it means the dataplane cannot
  show the removal on 232/8 — so the removal was proved a different way, and that attempt is
  what found F-14:

    (with NO SSM range configured at all)
    v2 (*,G) join on 232.7.7.7     -> swi_a#show ip igmp groups : NOT listed.  Still rejected.
    (then `access-list 20 permit 239.20.20.20 0.0.0.0` + `ip pim ssm range 20` on BOTH)
    v2 (*,G) join on 239.20.20.20  -> 239.20.20.20  vlan48  00:00:07  00:04:14  0.0.0.0
                                      ACCEPTED, inside the configured SSM range
    v2 (*,G) join on 232.7.7.7     -> still NOT listed, 40 s later, same table read:
    swi_a#show ip igmp groups
    Group Address    Interface            Uptime   Expires  Last Reporter
    239.20.20.20     vlan48               00:01:07 00:03:14 0.0.0.0        <- configured range
    (232.7.7.7 absent)                                                     <- IANA range

  => **F-14: the IGMPv2 any-source-join rejection is bound to the hardcoded IANA 232/8 range
     only, and is NOT applied to a range configured with `ip pim ssm range <acl>`.**  Measured
     on one router, one interface, two groups, in a single `show ip igmp groups`, and the
     rejection on 232/8 persists even with no SSM configuration at all — so `ip pim ssm default`
     is not what enforces it.  RFC 4607 §4.2 expects a router to apply SSM semantics to its
     configured range too.  **Impact is limited and was measured**: the accepted (*,G)
     membership is inert — traffic from an arbitrary source to 239.20.20.20 was **0 of 10** in
     the dataplane (cap s10-ssmrange-starg-eth4.pcap, 10/10 instrument control), because no RP
     is configured, so nothing leaks.  It is a spec-conformance gap that makes
     `ip pim ssm range` weaker than `ip pim ssm default`, not a forwarding hole.
     **Worth a developer's eye.**
  (The ACL and the range were then removed and `ip pim ssm default` restored.)
  VERDICT leg 6: PASS as a measurement; produced F-14 and O-27.

